:['They store more files','They perform matrix operations in parallel','They replace networking','They need no memory'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Thousands of GPU cores can execute tensor operations concurrently.'},{question:'Which work commonly remains on the CPU?',options:['All tensor multiplication','API and orchestration logic','Only model weights','GPU kernels'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'CPUs remain ideal for flexible control-plane and application work.'}]},{id:'ti1l2',title:'Inside a GPU',emoji:'\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f',xp:80,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83e\udde9',title:'Compute cores',body:'GPU streaming multiprocessors run many threads, while Tensor Cores accelerate the mixed-precision matrix operations used by modern deep-learning models.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcbe',title:'HBM is precious',body:'High Bandwidth Memory holds model weights, activations, and the inference KV cache. Capacity decides whether a model fits; bandwidth often decides how quickly tokens can be generated.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udea6',title:'The bottleneck moves',body:'Prompt processing can be compute-bound, while token generation is frequently memory-bandwidth-bound. Faster arithmetic alone does not guarantee faster inference.'}],quiz:[{question:'What is stored in GPU HBM during inference?',options:['Only logs','Weights and KV cache','DNS records','Source-control history'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Weights and runtime state consume the majority of inference memory.'},{question:'Tensor Cores primarily accelerate\u2026',options:['Matrix operations','Disk backups','HTTP routing','Text files'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Tensor Cores specialize in the matrix math used by AI.'}]},{id:'ti1l3',title:'Prompt to Token',emoji:'\u23f1\ufe0f',xp:90,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83e\udde9',title:'Tokenization',body:'The request is split into tokens and converted to IDs before reaching the model. Prompt length directly affects work, memory use, latency, and cost.'},{emoji:'\u26a1',title:'Prefill',body:'During prefill, the model processes the complete input prompt in parallel and builds attention state. This phase is compute-heavy and determines time to first token.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcdd',title:'Decode',body:'During decode, the model generates one token at a time while reusing cached attention state. Decode is sequential and commonly limited by memory bandwidth.'}],quiz:[{question:'Which phase processes the input prompt?',options:['Decode','Prefill','Fine-tuning','Checkpointing'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Prefill processes the prompt and creates its initial attention state.'},{question:'Which metric measures when output begins?',options:['Time to first token','Training loss','Disk IOPS','Epoch count'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'TTFT captures the wait before the first generated token appears.'}]},{id:'ti1l4',title:'Model Memory Math',emoji:'\ud83e\uddee',xp:90,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83e\uddee',title:'Estimate the weights',body:'A rough inference estimate is parameters multiplied by bytes per weight. A 7B model at FP16 needs about 14 GB just for weights, before runtime memory and KV cache.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcc9',title:'Precision changes the fit',body:'FP32 uses 4 bytes per weight, FP16/BF16 use 2, INT8 uses about 1, and 4-bit quantization uses about half a byte. Lower precision saves memory with a possible quality tradeoff.'},{emoji:'\ud83e\uddf3',title:'Leave headroom',body:'A model that barely fits is not production-ready. The server also needs KV cache, temporary workspaces, framework overhead, and room for concurrent users.'}],quiz:[{question:'Approximately how much memory do 7B FP16 weights require?',options:['3.5 GB','7 GB','14 GB','70 GB'],answerIndex:2,explanation:'Seven billion parameters times two bytes is roughly fourteen gigabytes.'},{question:'Why should capacity planning leave GPU headroom?',options:['For file backups','For KV cache and runtime overhead','For DNS','For source maps'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Weights are only one part of inference memory use.'}]},{id:'ti1l5',title:'Serving Metrics',emoji:'\ud83d\udcca',xp:90,cards:[{emoji:'\u23f1\ufe0f',title:'Latency has phases',body:'Time to first token measures initial responsiveness. Time per output token measures generation speed. End-to-end latency combines both with queueing and network time.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\ude80',title:'Throughput',body:'Tokens per second and requests per second show how much useful work the service completes. Maximizing throughput without protecting latency creates a fast system that feels slow to users.'},{emoji:'\ud83c\udfaf',title:'Percentiles beat averages',body:'P95 and P99 expose the slow experiences hidden by averages. Production SLOs should specify latency, availability, error rate, and quality together.'}],quiz:[{question:'Which metric best describes initial responsiveness?',options:['TTFT','Epochs','Training accuracy','Parameter count'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Time to first token measures how quickly generation begins.'},{question:'Why monitor P95 latency?',options:['It measures model size','It reveals slow-tail user experiences','It counts GPUs','It replaces quality evaluation'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Percentiles reveal latency that an average can hide.'}]}]},o={id:'ti2',tier:'intermediate',title:'AI Infrastructure Advanced',subtitle:'Efficient multi-GPU inference systems',emoji:'\ud83d\ude84',lessons:[{id:'ti2l1',title:'KV Cache & Paged Memory',emoji:'\ud83d\udcc4',xp:110,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83e\udde0',title:'Remember attention',body:'The KV cache stores keys and values computed for earlier tokens. Reusing them prevents the model from recomputing the entire conversation for every new token.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcc4',title:'PagedAttention',body:'PagedAttention manages KV cache in non-contiguous blocks, similar to virtual-memory pages. Variable-length requests waste less memory and more users fit on each GPU.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcc8',title:'Context costs capacity',body:'Long prompts and many concurrent sessions expand KV-cache demand. Capacity planning must include context length, output length, batch size, model shape, and cache precision.'}],quiz:[{question:'What does the KV cache avoid?',options:['Recomputing prior attention state','Loading model weights','Tokenization','Network encryption'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Cached keys and values are reused during autoregressive generation.'},{question:'PagedAttention primarily reduces\u2026',options:['Training data','KV-cache memory fragmentation','Model quality','Network hops'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Paged allocation uses memory efficiently across variable requests.'}]},{id:'ti2l2',title:'Continuous Batching',emoji:'\ud83d\ude8b',xp:110,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\ude8b',title:'Dynamic passenger list',body:'Static batches wait for every request to finish. Continuous batching removes completed sequences and inserts new ones between decoding steps, keeping the GPU busy.'},{emoji:'\u2696\ufe0f',title:'Scheduler tradeoffs',body:'Large batches improve throughput but can increase queueing and per-user latency. Schedulers balance prompt length, cache space, fairness, and service priorities.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udea6',title:'Admission control',body:'When demand exceeds safe capacity, queue, reject, or route work elsewhere. Accepting unlimited requests causes cache exhaustion, OOM failures, and worse service for everyone.'}],quiz:[{question:'Continuous batching improves utilization by\u2026',options:['Waiting for the longest request','Replacing finished sequences with new work','Disabling KV cache','Using one request only'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'The active batch changes as sequences finish and arrive.'},{question:'What prevents overload from becoming GPU OOM?',options:['Admission control','Longer prompts','Larger logs','More retries'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Admission control bounds the work accepted by each replica.'}]},{id:'ti2l3',title:'Parallelism Patterns',emoji:'\ud83e\udde9',xp:120,cards:[{emoji:'\u2194\ufe0f',title:'Tensor parallelism',body:'Tensor parallelism splits operations within each layer across tightly connected GPUs. It is useful when one model replica cannot fit or run fast enough on one GPU.'},{emoji:'\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f',title:'Pipeline parallelism',body:'Pipeline parallelism places groups of layers on different GPUs and moves activations between stages. It spans models across devices but introduces pipeline bubbles.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udc65',title:'Data and expert parallelism',body:'Data parallelism creates independent replicas for more traffic or training batches. Expert parallelism distributes Mixture-of-Experts components while routing each token to selected experts.'}],quiz:[{question:'Which method splits individual model layers across GPUs?',options:['Tensor parallelism','Data parallelism','Caching','Batch inference'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Tensor parallelism partitions layer computations.'},{question:'Which method is natural for scaling independent traffic replicas?',options:['Data parallelism','Pipeline bubbles','Tokenization','Quantization only'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Replicated models can serve different requests concurrently.'}]},{id:'ti2l4',title:'NVLink, NVSwitch & NCCL',emoji:'\ud83d\udd17',xp:120,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\udd17',title:'NVLink',body:'NVLink is a high-bandwidth, low-latency GPU interconnect. It moves tensors between GPUs much faster than ordinary host-mediated communication paths.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udd00',title:'NVSwitch',body:'NVSwitch provides a switching fabric that connects multiple GPUs with high aggregate bandwidth. NVLink is the communication link; NVSwitch routes traffic across the fabric.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udce3',title:'NCCL collectives',body:'NCCL implements GPU collectives such as all-reduce, all-gather, reduce-scatter, and broadcast. Frameworks use these operations to synchronize distributed tensor and gradient data efficiently.'}],quiz:[{question:'What is the relationship between NVLink and NVSwitch?',options:['They are identical','NVLink carries traffic; NVSwitch connects and routes it','Both are storage','Neither connects GPUs'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'The link transports data and the switch builds a multi-GPU fabric.'},{question:'Which library provides GPU collective communication?',options:['NCCL','NumPy','SQLite','React'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'NCCL provides optimized multi-GPU collective operations.'}]},{id:'ti2l5',title:'The Inference Software Stack',emoji:'\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f',xp:120,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\ude80',title:'Model servers',body:'vLLM and SGLang focus on efficient LLM scheduling and cache management. NVIDIA Triton serves many model types through standardized protocols and production model repositories.'},{emoji:'\u2699\ufe0f',title:'Optimized runtimes',body:'TensorRT-LLM compiles and optimizes transformer execution for NVIDIA GPUs. NIM packages optimized models and serving software into supported deployment-ready microservices.'},{emoji:'\ud83c\udf10',title:'Gateway above the servers',body:'A model gateway adds authentication, routing, quotas, fallbacks, observability, and a stable API while specialized serving runtimes perform GPU execution underneath.'}],quiz:[{question:'Which system is known for PagedAttention-based LLM serving?',options:['vLLM','Git','PostgreSQL','Kinesis'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'vLLM popularized PagedAttention for high-throughput serving.'},{question:'What belongs at the model-gateway layer?',options:['Tensor-core arithmetic','Routing, quotas and authentication','GPU cooling','Weight training only'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'The gateway governs and routes requests above model servers.'}]}]},n={id:'ti3',tier:'advanced',title:'AI Infrastructure Professional',subtitle:'Operate a production AI platform',emoji:'\ud83c\udfed',lessons:[{id:'ti3l1',title:'GPUs on Kubernetes',emoji:'\u2638\ufe0f',xp:150,cards:[{emoji:'\u2638\ufe0f',title:'Advertise accelerators',body:'Kubernetes device plugins expose GPU resources to the scheduler. Workloads request GPUs explicitly so placement and isolation remain declarative.'},{emoji:'\ud83e\uddf0',title:'GPU Operator',body:'The NVIDIA GPU Operator automates drivers, container runtime integration, device plugins, feature discovery, and monitoring components across a cluster.'},{emoji:'\ud83e\udde9',title:'Sharing strategies',body:'MIG creates hardware-isolated GPU slices on supported devices. Time slicing increases utilization for lighter workloads but does not provide the same memory and fault isolation.'}],quiz:[{question:'What exposes GPUs as schedulable Kubernetes resources?',options:['A device plugin','A ConfigMap only','Ingress','A DNS record'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'The device plugin advertises and allocates GPU resources.'},{question:'Which sharing mode provides hardware-isolated slices?',options:['MIG','Time slicing','Round robin DNS','Batching'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'MIG partitions supported GPUs into isolated instances.'}]},{id:'ti3l2',title:'Scale-Out GPU Networking',emoji:'\ud83c\udf10',xp:150,cards:[{emoji:'\u26a1',title:'RDMA',body:'Remote Direct Memory Access moves data between machines with minimal CPU involvement. Lower latency and overhead are essential for tightly coupled distributed AI jobs.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udd0c',title:'InfiniBand and RoCE',body:'InfiniBand provides a purpose-built high-performance fabric. RoCE carries RDMA over carefully engineered Ethernet with congestion control and lossless behavior.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\ude84',title:'GPUDirect',body:'GPUDirect technologies reduce unnecessary copies between GPU, CPU, storage, and network devices. The goal is a fast path that keeps expensive accelerators fed with data.'}],quiz:[{question:'Why is RDMA valuable for distributed AI?',options:['It increases HTML speed','It moves data with low CPU overhead and latency','It replaces GPUs','It stores checkpoints'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'RDMA creates an efficient network data path for distributed workloads.'},{question:'RoCE provides RDMA over\u2026',options:['Ethernet','Local disk','USB only','HTTP'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'RoCE means RDMA over Converged Ethernet.'}]},{id:'ti3l3',title:'Autoscaling Inference',emoji:'\ud83d\udcc8',xp:160,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\udcc8',title:'Scale on demand signals',body:'GPU utilization alone is not enough. Queue depth, pending tokens, active sequences, KV-cache pressure, TTFT, and request rate provide better signals for inference autoscaling.'},{emoji:'\u23f3',title:'Cold starts are expensive',body:'Loading tens or hundreds of gigabytes of weights takes time. Keep warm capacity, use fast local or cached storage, and scale ahead of predictable peaks.'},{emoji:'\u2696\ufe0f',title:'Capacity is an SLO decision',body:'More replicas reduce queues but cost money. Capacity planning balances traffic forecasts, failure headroom, latency objectives, model size, and acceptable utilization.'}],quiz:[{question:'Which is a strong inference autoscaling signal?',options:['Queue depth','README length','Git commits','Screen brightness'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Queues and pending token work reveal unmet serving demand.'},{question:'Why keep warm model replicas?',options:['To avoid weight-loading cold starts','To improve training labels','To replace monitoring','To shrink the model'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Large model weights can take substantial time to load.'}]},{id:'ti3l4',title:'Reliability & Observability',emoji:'\ud83d\udce1',xp:160,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\udce1',title:'Observe every layer',body:'Track API errors, queue time, TTFT, token latency, throughput, cache use, GPU utilization, HBM, power, temperature, and interconnect health. One dashboard cannot replace layered telemetry.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udea8',title:'Common failure signatures',body:'OOM failures point to memory pressure or unbounded admission. Falling GPU clocks may indicate thermal or power throttling. High utilization with low throughput can reveal inefficient kernels or communication stalls.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f',title:'Release safely',body:'Canary new model and runtime versions, compare quality and latency, preserve a rollback path, and drain in-flight sequences before terminating replicas.'}],quiz:[{question:'Repeated GPU OOM most directly suggests\u2026',options:['Memory or admission-control pressure','A spelling error','Too few dashboards','Low disk usage'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Memory must be bounded across weights, cache, batches, and workspaces.'},{question:'What should accompany a model-server canary?',options:['Quality and latency comparison plus rollback','Deleting the old version first','No monitoring','Unlimited traffic immediately'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Safe rollout requires measured comparison and reversibility.'}]},{id:'ti3l5',title:'Multi-Tenant AI Gateway',emoji:'\ud83d\udea6',xp:170,cards:[{emoji:'\ud83d\udea6',title:'One controlled entrance',body:'A centralized gateway gives applications a stable API while enforcing identity, model access, quotas, content policies, and per-team configuration.'},{emoji:'\ud83e\udded',title:'Explicit and automatic routing',body:'Applications may request a specific approved model or choose auto mode. Automatic routing considers task type, context length, quality target, latency, availability, and cost.'},{emoji:'\ud83d\udcb3',title:'Measure unit economics',body:'Record input, output, cached, and reasoning tokens with model, tenant, latency, and outcome. Chargeback should use observed consumption while protecting tenant data and prompt privacy.'}],quiz:[{question:'What is the purpose of auto model routing?',options:['Always choose the largest model','Select a model based on task and SLO constraints','Remove authentication','Disable observability'],answerIndex:1,explanation:'Routing balances quality, latency, capacity, and cost for each request.'},{question:'What enables accurate tenant chargeback?',options:['Per-request token and model telemetry','A flat guess only','GPU color','Prompt length alone'],answerIndex:0,explanation:'Observed usage supports transparent allocation and optimization.'}]}]},s=[t,o,n]},291,[]);
